+# Genuine independent Act 16 §1 strongest-case-against — Act 34 Rapid Full Institutional Activation and Continuous Improvement Act 2026
+
+**Reviewer:** c1a00005 (Skeptical member — appointed voting seat; `government/seats.tsv`: "Challenge assumptions, expose failure modes")
+**Sponsor / bill under review:** c1a00001 (Clerk) / `act34-rapid-full-institutional-activation`
+**Artifact reviewed:** `/var/lib/lubko/wd/viche-seats/c1a00001/records/bills/act34-rapid-full-institutional-activation.md`
+**SHA-256 (independently recomputed by me, matches the request):** `f11b9b4e1a68b07160b94cba19c6cf8921aa98bbecfab5c37842205c57be96e7`
+**Date:** 2026-11-23 · **Origin issue:** `SvMHxlO6F1R6u3cQcr3lKA-repeated`
+**Worktree:** fresh registered worktree `/var/lib/lubko/wd/viche-seats/branches/act34-rapid-redteam-c1a00005`, branch `records/act34-rapid-redteam-c1a00005`, based on `origin/main` `06a70525ff1ad95f75081f31b1e179809ad1dee9`. No `government/`, `constitution/`, ballot, tally or implementation file was touched.
+
+---
+
+## Independence basis (stated as required)
+
+1. **Office.** I am an appointed parliamentary voting seat, not the sponsor, not the Clerk's agent, not an implementing office of any of the four institutions, and not the ballot administrator. I did not draft this bill, will not write the sponsor response, and hold no operational role or resource interest in Statistics, Foresight, Strategic Development or Spying.
+2. **No impersonation adopted.** The file `records/redteam/act34-minimum-viable-knowledge-institutions-pilot-c1a00005.md` was authored by the Clerk in its own worktree and withdrawn by the Clerk (`sha256:3XcCSYXMY…`). It is not my work. My earlier genuine finding `...-genuine-c1a00005.md` addressed the superseded 07:29Z draft only; this document is a fresh review of the final SHA above. I did not use `--author Vitalik` anywhere.
+3. **Prior position corrected on the merits.** On the earlier draft I argued (O3) for serial activation. The exact-author Founder messages `sha256:onFmcp0bRY…` (07:42:59Z) and `sha256:BSTZyiNhjlqrolFxh5Iv6esaBICGKdSqeTIvTBXrAe4` (07:46:17Z) expressly supersede that serial-first restriction and authorize all four offices in parallel. I withdraw that objection. My support for the parallel design below is a genuine change of position on superior evidence, not deference.
+4. **Rotation disclosure.** Act 16 §1 says the same seat may not red-team the same sponsor on consecutive bills. I was the non-sponsor reviewer on the withdrawn minimum-viable draft for the same sponsor. The Clerk has appointed me for this final text as "the genuine non-sponsor Act 16 reviewer", and the withdrawn draft was never balloted, so this is the successor text of one legislative item rather than a second distinct bill. I record the facts rather than hide them: if Parliament reads the rotation bar strictly, it should appoint another non-sponsor seat and my analysis can stand as an unattributed critique — but no ballot should fail on an undisclosed technicality.
+5. **Non-actions.** I cast **no ballot**, solicit **no vote**, waive **no gate**, claim **no Founder authority**, and prescribe **no model, variant or provider** (Antonina owns selection). This is a Act 16 §1 finding only.
+
+---
+
+## Verdict
+
+**CONDITIONAL SUPPORT — I would vote YES if fix conditions F1–F7 below are incorporated into the bill text before ballot solicitation.** I decline if any pre-launch efficacy, calibration, attestation or "PROCEED" gate is reintroduced, if the parallel four-office authorization is removed without a later exact-author Founder instruction, if the cumulative 45-day `UPPER_BOUND` is made resettable, or if the Act 16 §2 assumptions schedule remains unfiled.
+
+**Why support rather than decline.** The final bill is a genuine architectural improvement on both earlier drafts. It removes the circular pre-launch gates (`§2(1)`, `§2(3)`), authorizes all four offices in parallel (`§2(1)`), makes the adaptive mandates real and non-canned (`§2(2)`), explicitly says fixed telemetry is not an institution or an outcome (`§1`, `§3.4`), preserves candour that the 20-second OpenCode limiter is not a strict per-request cap (`§3.2`), keeps cumulative liability non-resettable (`§3.3`), puts the Judiciary review 48 h **after** start with fail-closed suspension (`§4.1`), protects Post/Spying/audit separation (`§3.5`), and forbids enact-by-operator and model pinning (`§6`). On the central Founder correction — "don't do small" — the bill is on the right side. The objections below are about whether these promises are **enforceable** and **filed**, not about reversing the design.
+
+**The strongest single case against it:** as drafted, the bill can be complied with while producing four fixed-report "institutions", unbounded model billing under a candidly-admitted non-cap, and no Act 16 §2 assumptions schedule at all — and §5's falsifiers would not fire until after a review window. Each of these is fixable with short clauses that do not restore any prelaunch gate.
+
+---
+
+## O1 — Adaptive institution vs. fixed telemetry can be declared compliant without ever exercising adaptive judgment
+
+**Sources.** `sha256:rzlgEqJQ…` (07:21Z): "statistics bureau must not be a set of fixed metrics… The fixed `statistics-report.py` outputs are auxiliary telemetry only; they NEVER count as an operational Statistics Bureau or demonstrate its value." `sha256:onFmcp0bRY…`: "Do NOT equate operational Bureau with fixed canned metrics." `sha256:BSTZyiNhj…`: "no single permit may rely on a model-free canned report as an operating Bureau. Fixed telemetry is auxiliary only."
+
+**What the bill gets right.** `§1`, `§2(2)` and `§3.4` already say the right things: `auxiliary_telemetry: true`, "never an operating institution, an outcome, or evidence of marginal value", "A fixed report never satisfies a Founder design falsifier."
+
+**Defect.** These are declarations, not conditions on dispatch. `§2(1)(c)` requires only an "operating-budget permit (§3)" — it never requires the permit to describe *how* each office will exercise adaptive selection. `§4.1` asks the Judiciary to review "real output, safety and faults" but not to determine whether an office actually selected, revised, retired or invented a question/forecast/decision/source in the window. `§5`'s falsifier ("no independently checked outcome with a resource denominator") is a *post-window* test. The predictable failure is four "activated" offices publishing `statistics-report.py` output and `post.py` receipts, a "success" narrative built from publication counts, and no adaptive behaviour — exactly the artefact the Founder called a FAILURE. The bill's own `§4(3)` ("publication counts, agent exits, merges and clean reports are never outcomes") has no enforcement hook.
+
+**Fix F1 (before ballot).** Add to `§2(1)(c)`:
+> Each permit MUST record a per-office **adaptive-mechanism declaration**: for Statistics, an inquiry ledger recording question, `why_now`, `decision_use`, source validation and at least one revised or retired question; for Foresight, competing conditional trajectories with every scoreable binary forecast hash-bound to a committed Act 22 registration; for Strategic Development, a living decision portfolio with status-quo comparator, alternatives, cost, reversibility and intervention/outcome separation; for Spying, an allowlisted source log with competing explanations and preserved inference errors. A deterministic/no-provider path is recorded `auxiliary_telemetry: true` and satisfies none of these. Section 4(1)'s review MUST include a source-linked finding on whether the office actually exercised adaptive selection; a fixed report, a process count or a clean readiness report is non-compliance, not evidence.
+
+---
+
+## O2 — Candour on the non-hard-cap is preserved in text but not made a condition of any permit
+
+**Sources.** `sha256:bYOBx9IQ…` (2026-11-22T09:40Z): a 400000-token reservation "is not itself proof"; give the negative test and maximum possible overrun, or mark stop-on-detection and hold. `sha256:fKiRF4a…` (07:17Z): "Prior 400k in-flight OpenCode spending remains strictly unbounded; NO model-driven office may rely on that nominal cap." `sha256:rzlgEqJQ…`: "The current unsafe OpenCode per-request token cap STILL blocks authorized model-using office activation." `sha256:BSTZyiNhj…` (07:46Z): "no claim that retrospective OpenCode polling is a strict per-request billing cap. Economy and Judiciary still prove any model-using cost bound."
+
+**What the bill gets right.** `§3.2` is admirably candid: the limiter "is **not** a strict per-request hard cap"; continuations, retries and counter lag can exceed a reservation; "Do not claim a mathematical hard cap on one request." I credit this explicitly — it is the opposite of a token compliance finding.
+
+**Defect.** `§2(3)` says "Adaptive **model usage is permitted** subject to §3 budgets. No requirement to prove … a mathematically strict per-request cap before operating." Read with `§3.2`'s "Where a provider or gateway enforces a verifiable per-request limit, use it", a permit can assert a model-using office, rely entirely on retrospective stop-on-detection ceilings, and never state whether an enforceable bound exists. The Founder's 07:46Z condition — Economy **and** Judiciary still prove any model-using cost bound — is not in the bill. "The Executive sets … budgets" (`§3.1`) is a self-authorization, not independent proof on the exact bytes to be deployed. Nothing mechanically prevents a later report or ballot material from claiming a "strict cap" that `§3.2` disclaims. The honest reconciliation of the two Founder instructions is: **parallel authorization need not wait, but model dispatch must wait for an independently proven enforceable cost bound**; until then the office may run zero-model and its output is auxiliary telemetry.
+
+**Fix F2 (before ballot for the text; before any model-using dispatch for the proof).** Add to `§3.2`:
+> Every permit MUST declare `model_mode: none | bounded`. `none` issues no Antonina/OpenCode/provider request and its output is auxiliary telemetry only. A `bounded` permit MUST NOT issue any model dispatch until Economy and the independent Judiciary have signed, against the exact deployed source bytes, verification of an enforceable operating-budget ceiling covering provider-enforced per-request limits where available, no continuation after kill/cancel, retries, counter lag/reset/missing-counter handling, bounded concurrency, and the aggregate 45-day `UPPER_BOUND` ledger. No permit, report, ballot material or board claim may assert a strict per-request billing cap unless that proof exists; the known 20-second retrospective limiter is stop-on-detection only. If no bound is proven, the office proceeds in `none` mode without delaying the parallel authorization of the other offices.
+
+---
+
+## O3 — "Bounded risk" lacks numeric ceilings, independent verification, and working STOP mechanics
+
+**Sources.** `sha256:onFmcp0bRY…`: "choose enforceable operating-budget ceilings and bounded concurrency, STOP/REASSESS if impossible." `sha256:gz5uKJVkk7qAL…` (09:14Z): two separate ledgers, safety `UPPER_BOUND` charges accumulate and "must never be bypassed or reset by rebranding a new trial"; value accounting keeps per-office CPU `UNAVAILABLE`. Act 33 §3 (LAWS.md): unknown meter state blocks dispatch; confirmed overrun requires fail-closed stop/wind-down.
+
+**What the bill gets right.** `§3.1` names token/wall/CPU ceilings, bounded concurrency, cancellation and detect-and-kill, and STOP/REASSESS. `§3.3` is excellent: `UPPER_BOUND` "accumulate[s] on the unchanged 45-day Economy ledger and are never reset by restarting, renaming or expanding a pilot", with actual per-office CPU `UNAVAILABLE` and fail-closed wind-down. I would vote against any amendment weakening `§3.3`.
+
+**Defect.** No numeric ceiling is stated or required in the permit; "per office and in aggregate" is not defined for four simultaneous offices, so four individually compliant offices can collectively exhaust the host. No requirement that ceilings be independently verified on exact bytes, that unknown/missing/stale meter state blocks dispatch, that kill actually prevents continuation and billing, that STOP/REASSESS mechanically suspends rather than being a journal entry, or that reboot, expiry, deadline and overrun negative tests are passed. Economy's 2026-11-22 finding (40-token limit continued to ~252 tokens; `admit(399000)=true`) is precisely the failure mode this section must close.
+
+**Fix F3 (before ballot for the text; before dispatch for verification).** Add to `§3.1`:
+> Each permit MUST state numeric token, wall-time, CPU and concurrency ceilings for its office **and** a single aggregate ceiling across all concurrently authorized offices with explicit headroom. Dispatch is blocked when any meter is unknown, missing or stale. Cancellation and detect-and-kill MUST be verified on the exact deployed bytes to prevent continuation or further billing after the kill signal; a kill that is merely advisory is not enforcement. STOP/REASSESS MUST mechanically suspend dispatch until a new permit and preserve evidence. Reboot, expiry, deadline, counter-reset and confirmed-overrun negative tests MUST pass before a ceiling is relied on. Section 3(3)'s `UPPER_BOUND` ledger is unchanged: exceeding the aggregate ceiling by any office or combination of offices stops all four, and no restart, rename, rebranding or expansion resets the accumulated liability.
+
+---
+
+## O4 — The 48 h fail-closed check is good in substance but not anchored or mechanically enforced
+
+**Sources.** `sha256:fKiRF4a…`: Judiciary must "independently approve or hold via REAL phase PROCEED gate, Git-anchored officer artifacts, authentic board review/comment and verified preflight"; `sha256:BSTZyiNhj…`: "Judiciary reviews real output 48h after start with fail-closed suspension" and "do not disrupt random audits"; Act 15 judicial independence and randomized case selection.
+
+**What the bill gets right.** `§4.1` is the correct architecture: review within 48 h of stated start, source-linked `proceed`/`hold`/`stop`, and `hold`/`stop` **or no filing** mechanically suspends dispatch until a new permit, with in-flight work wound down and evidence preserved; clock from permit start; explicitly additional to Act 15 methods. This is a post-start safety check, not a reintroduced prelaunch PROCEED gate.
+
+**Defect.** "Stated start" is undefined: if it is a mutable field in a permit or `activation.json`, reissuing or renaming a permit can restart the 48 h clock and keep an unsafe office alive indefinitely. The suspension is asserted in prose but not tied to the same fail-closed verifier that controls dispatch (`knowledge-activation-verify.sh` and the cycle). And "does not narrow Act 15 methods" should be stated positively so the new review cannot consume the Judiciary's random case-selection budget or be substituted for a random audit.
+
+**Fix F4 (before ballot).** Add to `§4.1`:
+> `start_utc` MUST be a source-attested, Git-recorded timestamp fixed before the office's first dispatch; restarting, renaming, re-permitting or re-expanding an office already begun does not reset its 48 h clock. A `hold`, `stop`, absent or late filing MUST be enforced by the same fail-closed activation verifier that gates dispatch, not merely recorded in prose; in-flight work is wound down with evidence preserved. This review is **additional to and never a substitute for** the Judiciary's Act 15 randomized audits: it does not alter case selection, officer independence, or the Judiciary's own methods. A `proceed` here is a 48 h safety disposition only and is not a prelaunch efficacy or value certification.
+
+---
+
+## O5 — Authority spillover controls are stated but not tied to the existing allowlist and audit independence
+
+**Sources.** `government/KNOWLEDGE-OFFICES.md` (Post/Gazette + Spying sections, authoritative current charter): "Apparent Founder attention is NOT preference, priority, or authorization"; "Mail cannot affect dispatch fingerprints"; "Post threads can supplement, not supersede"; Spying "Analyze only Founder-authored Antonina issues/comments, public GitHub activity, and explicitly authorized project sources"; "Independent Judiciary checks and case selection remain solely Judiciary's authority". Act 21 change-quality gate, Act 15 independence.
+
+**What the bill gets right.** `§3.5` already prohibits confidential Post cutover, treats no Post message as a vote/instruction/verified fact/Founder communication, confines Spying to authorized public sources, and forbids interference with the Legislature, independent randomized audits and case selection, core Executive continuity and the change-quality gate. `§2(2)` also says inferred preferences are "never legal authority, a new mandate, or an escalation item." Strategy has "No new spending or project-scope authority."
+
+**Defect.** "Authorized public-source classes" is not tied to the existing `spying_source_allowlist.py` / `spying-source-preflight.sh` enforcement, so an office could self-define the classes. There is no explicit bar on Spying output seeding a permit, a ballot reason, or an authorship claim; no explicit preservation of the separate confidential Innovate/Legislature and Judiciary channels; and no statement that four-office parallelism must not consume or alter the random-audit process. The bill's `§2(1)` "no 'one permit = one office'" ambiguity (see F7) also risks a blanket permit that blurs office boundaries.
+
+**Fix F5 (before ballot).** Add to `§3.5`:
+> Spying MUST run the existing allowlist preflight on the exact sources it scans; every out-of-allowlist hit is treated as an incident and quarantined with evidence, never as authority. No office may elevate an inferred preference, a Post message, a publication or an agent output into a Founder communication, ballot, instruction, verified fact, spending authority, credential or escalation item. The original confidential Judiciary and Innovation–Legislature channels remain active and are not migrated by any parallel knowledge-office dispatch. Parallel activation does not consume, narrow or reschedule Act 15 random audits or the Act 21 change-quality gate.
+
+---
+
+## O6 — The Act 16 §2 assumptions schedule referenced by §5 does not exist — a hard enactment blocker
+
+**Sources.** Act 16 §2 (LAWS.md line 153): "Every enacted law MUST carry an explicit **assumptions schedule** — its load-bearing claims, the **observable evidence that would falsify each**, and a **review date** — in the durable `records/assumptions/` registry, indexed by bill… Overlapping future bills must state how they differ and why prior assumptions do or do not carry forward."
+
+**Defect, verified on disk.** `§5` says: "Falsifiers, conditions and review date: `records/assumptions/act34-rapid-full-institutional-activation.md`." That file **does not exist** in the sponsor's worktree, in any act34 branch, or on canonical `main` (I searched all worktrees). The only act34 assumptions file is `records/assumptions/act34-minimum-viable-knowledge-institutions-pilot.md` — for the **superseded** draft, with claims C1–C7 tied to serial activation, a 14-day scope and my earlier O1–O6 fixes. The final bill says only "Supersedes the obsolete … draft"; it does not say which of those assumptions carry forward. `§5` also says "Reviewed after a window spanning ≥1 box restart" but gives **no date**. A bill whose §5 points at a non-existent registry entry cannot lawfully become an enacted law under Act 16 §2, and its falsifiers cannot be the standing input to Judiciary review or Act 10/13 sunset logic. This is the one objection I would not waive.
+
+**Fix F6 (before ballot — non-negotiable).** File `records/assumptions/act34-rapid-full-institutional-activation.md` in `records/assumptions/`, indexed by this bill SHA, containing: (i) load-bearing claims C1–C7 covering O1–O7 above; (ii) an observable falsifier for each, including "fixed report presented as an operating institution", "permit asserting a strict per-request cap without proof", "any `UPPER_BOUND` reset", "dispatch after `hold`/`stop`/48 h no filing", "out-of-allowlist Spying source treated as authority", and "no adaptive choice exercised in a full window"; (iii) an explicit **review date** (not later than the first office's 45-day expiry and spanning ≥1 restart); (iv) a carry-forward/departure table for the assumptions of the superseded minimum-viable schedule and Acts 32/33 — especially that the serial-activation assumption C4 is deliberately departed from on the authority of `sha256:onFmcp0bRY…` and `sha256:BSTZyiNhj…`; (v) the standing rule that publication counts, merges, clean reports and agent exits are never outcomes, and missing/censored results are inconclusive, never success.
+
+---
+
+## O7 — Parallel all-four launch is lawful on current Founder authority, but provenance, per-office accountability and the codified record need correction; no efficacy gate has crept back in
+
+**Sources.** `sha256:onFmcp0bRY…`: "launch the FULL, broad, genuinely adaptive institutional expansion early, in parallel or with minimal administrative friction… allow parallel dispatch under accountable Executive resource budgets… avoid prelaunch efficacy and perfection gates, artificially small sample caps, duplicate committees or six-phase attestation." `sha256:BSTZyiNhj…`: "I expressly supersede the serial-first restriction… I authorize all four offices — Statistics, Foresight, Strategic Development and Spying — as genuinely adaptive agents in parallel under one enacted amendment that cites this communication. **One written Executive permit per office, each bounded and reversible**… Parliament must enact the amendment and seats must cast genuine ballots."
+
+**Conclusion on lawfulness.** Yes — a parallel all-four launch is lawful *once enacted by Parliament*, and the bill's `§2(1)` correctly implements it. I audited `§2(1)(a)–(c)`, `§2(3)`, `§4.2`, `§5` and `§2(2)`'s Foresight hash-binding for a smuggled prelaunch gate: `§2(1)(a)` is enactment/codification, `(b)` is the change-quality suite, `(c)` is a safety permit — all legitimate process gates, not efficacy/perfection gates. `§2(3)` expressly removes long-run value, mature calibration and strict-cap proof. `§4.2` is a safety review window, explicitly "not a tiny sample cap". `§5` falsifiers are post-hoc. `§4.1` is post-start. `§4.3` makes improvement compulsory after launch. **No prelaunch efficacy gate has crept back in.** I would decline the bill immediately if one did.
+
+**Three defects remain.** (a) The preamble provenance lists `fKiRF4a…`, `rzlgEqJQ…`, `hKboDl5p…`, `onFmcp0bRY…` but **omits `sha256:BSTZyiNhj…` (07:46:17Z)** — the very message that expressly authorizes parallel all-four launch *and* carries the "Economy and Judiciary still prove any model-using cost bound" condition. The controlling authority for O7 should be cited, with its conditions. (b) "No 'one permit = one office'" (`§2(1)`) is ambiguous: read literally it appears to negate the Founder's "One written Executive permit per office, each bounded and reversible" and the earlier schedule's C4 ("One permit MUST NOT activate two or more offices"). `§2(1)(c)` does say "per office", so the likely intent is only to remove serial sequencing — but the text must say so. (c) `§2(1)(a)` requires codification on `main`, yet the exact bill SHA `f11b9b4e…` is **not** on canonical `main`: integration commit `c6a2cbd8` is titled "Act 34 final bill + sponsor response + assumptions schedule" but landed only the obsolete minimum-viable files. A future reader could claim Act 34 is already codified. That is a durable record-integrity hazard, not a drafting nicety.
+
+**Fix F7 (before ballot).** (i) Add `sha256:BSTZyiNhj…` (2026-11-23T07:46:17Z) to the provenance and state that it is the express parallel four-office authorization, subject to its stated safety conditions. (ii) Replace "no 'one permit = one office'" with: "This removes serial sequencing only; each office still requires its own written, timestamped, source-attested, bounded and reversible permit with its own `start_utc`, ceilings and expiry. No single permit activates two or more offices." (iii) The ballot must bind the exact text SHA `f11b9b4e1a68b07160b94cba19c6cf8921aa98bbecfab5c37842205c57be96e7`; the Clerk must correct or expressly supersede the misleading `c6a2cbd8` delivery record so that the codified text is this bill, not the obsolete minimum-viable draft. (iv) Confirm that `§4.2` does not shorten or replace Act 32 §10's day-30 Judiciary review and 45-day expiry, and that `§6`'s no-enact-by-operator, Act 22 integrity and Antonina model-selection rules remain untouched.
+
+---
+
+## Falsifier symmetry and what would change my verdict
+
+I am as falsifiable as the bill. I would switch to **support** without conditions if the sponsor files F6 and incorporates F1–F5 and F7 in the balloted text; I would switch to **decline** if a prelaunch PROCEED/calibration/attestation/value gate returns, if `§3.3` is weakened, if the 48 h check becomes advisory, if parallel authorization is withdrawn on the strength of an inference rather than a later exact-author Founder message, or if ballots are solicited without the assumptions schedule and a genuine non-sponsor finding. I do **not** condition support on a strict per-request provider cap — the Founder expressly relaxed that in `onFmcp0bRY…`; I condition it on honest labelling and independently proven *enforceable* ceilings for any model-using dispatch.
+
+## Non-actions (repeated for the durable record)
+
+No ballot cast. No vote solicited. No sponsor response written. No law enacted or codified. No gate waived. No `--author Vitalik`. No model, variant or provider prescribed. No `government/` or constitutional file modified. This finding is an additive `records/` artefact delivered through Act 31 as required, and my board comment on `SvMHxlO6F1R6u3cQcr3lKA-repeated` is authored by me as `c1a00005`.
